\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Transfer and the effect of dimension}

\p{The [orientation character](frap-000A) cancels changes of local frames.
The same algebra appears in finite-index transfer: a group element permutes
cosets, leaving a residual element in the stabilizer at each step. We derive
that construction and identify exactly how it applies to a puzzle action.
Marchetti points to this relationship in the discussion of orientation
characters \citet{Section 3.1}{marchetti2025rubik}.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Theorem}
  \title{Finite-index transfer}
  \p{Let #{H\leq G} have finite index, and choose representatives #{r_i}
  for the left cosets #{r_iH}, indexed by a finite set #{I}. For #{g\in G},
  uniquely write}
  ##{g r_i=r_{\pi_g(i)}h_i(g),\qquad h_i(g)\in H.}
  \p{The map #{T:G\to H_{\mathrm{ab}}} given by
  #{T(g)=\prod_i[h_i(g)]} is a homomorphism independent of the chosen
  representatives.}
  \proof{
    \p{Multiplication on cosets gives #{\pi_{gk}=\pi_g\pi_k}. Expanding
    #{gkr_i} in two steps yields}
    ##{h_i(gk)=h_{\pi_k(i)}(g)h_i(k).}
    \p{Taking classes and multiplying over #{i} gives #{T(gk)=T(g)T(k)},
    since #{\pi_k} just reorders the first factors. Replace #{r_i} by
    #{r_i t_i}, #{t_i\in H}. The new residual factor is
    #{t_{\pi_g(i)}^{-1}h_i(g)t_i}. Its total class has each #{[t_i]}
    and its inverse exactly once, leaving #{T(g)}. Reordering the cosets
    also merely reorders an abelian product.}
  }
}

\p{This is the usual transfer with left cosets and left actions. It requires
finite index, not that #{G} itself be finite. The target is the abelianization
of the subgroup #{H}, not the abelianization of #{G}.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Proposition}
  \title{Transfer computes a total local orientation}
  \p{Suppose #{G} acts transitively on a finite set of positions #{I} and
  acts on local fibers compatibly. Choose #{i_0\in I}, its stabilizer
  #{H=G_{i_0}}, and a frame in the fiber at #{i_0}. Suppose the action
  there gives a homomorphism #{\rho:H\to K}. Choose #{r_i\in G} taking
  #{i_0} to #{i}, and use the transported base frame at each position.
  Then the orientation character of this action is}
  ##{\chi=\rho_{\mathrm{ab}}\circ T_{G,H}.}
  \proof{
    \p{The positions identify with left cosets #{G/H}: two elements
    carry #{i_0} to the same position exactly when they differ on the
    right by an element of #{H}. The equality
    #{g r_i=r_{\pi_g(i)}h_i(g)} says that the local framed action is
    #{\rho(h_i(g))}. Its total class is
    #{\prod_i[\rho(h_i(g))]=\rho_{\mathrm{ab}}(T(g))}. The induced
    map on abelianizations exists because a homomorphism sends
    commutators to commutators.}
  }
}

\p{For the ordinary cube, take #{G} to be the actual group generated by
outer-face turns, acting on corners. These turns are transitive on the eight
corner positions: adjacent corners share a face whose quarter-turn takes
one to the other, and the cube's edge graph is connected. Thus a corner
stabilizer #{H} has index eight. Its action on the three cyclically ordered
stickers of that corner gives #{\rho:H\to C_3}. The displayed identity
identifies the twist character with this transfer followed by
#{\rho_{\mathrm{ab}}}, without presupposing a classification of #{G}.}

\p{For edges the index is twelve and the local group is #{C_2}. To see
transitivity, the four edges of a face form one orbit under its rotation;
neighboring faces share an edge, and the graph of cube faces is connected.
The corresponding orbit sets therefore join all twelve edges. The preceding
note proves that both characters vanish on legal generators. This does not
say that the transfers themselves vanish in the larger stabilizer
abelianizations, only that their images under the stated local actions do.}

\p{Now vary abstract dimension rather than layer count. A vertex figure of
a #{d}-simplex or a #{d}-cube is a #{(d-1)}-simplex with #{d} vertices.
Its automorphisms permute these vertices, giving #{S_d}, and its rotations
are the even permutations #{A_d}. Indeed the distinguished involutions
are adjacent transpositions, so even words give precisely #{A_d}.
This local rotation group is the corner factor appearing in the abstract
orientation construction \citet{Section 3.1}{marchetti2025rubik}.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Theorem}
  \title{Three dimensions, four dimensions, and beyond}
  \p{The abelianizations of these local rotation groups are}
  ##{(A_3)_{\mathrm{ab}}\cong C_3,\qquad
      (A_4)_{\mathrm{ab}}\cong C_3,\qquad
      (A_d)_{\mathrm{ab}}=1\quad(d\geq5).}
  \proof{
    \p{The group #{A_3} is cyclic of order three, so already abelian.
    In #{A_4}, let
    #{V=\{1,(12)(34),(13)(24),(14)(23)\}}. Products and inverses stay
    in this four-element set, and conjugation permutes its three
    nonidentity elements. Hence #{V} is normal. The quotient has order
    three, so it is cyclic and #{[A_4,A_4]\subseteq V}. With our convention
    #{[g,h]=ghg^{-1}h^{-1}}, direct multiplication gives
    #{[(123),(124)]=(12)(34)}. Conjugating this element by the three-cycle
    #{(123)} gives the other two double transpositions. The commutator
    subgroup is normal, so it contains all of #{V}; equality follows.}
    \p{For #{d\geq5}, put #{g=(12)(34)} and #{h=(12)(35)} in #{A_d}.
    Multiplication gives #{[g,h]=(345)}. Every three-cycle is conjugate
    to this one within #{A_d}: first choose a conjugating permutation in
    #{S_d}; if it is odd, compose on the right with a transposition of
    two symbols outside #{\{3,4,5\}}, which centralizes #{(345)} and
    changes the parity. Thus the normal subgroup #{[A_d,A_d]} contains
    every three-cycle.}
    \p{Finally, three-cycles generate all even permutations. Write an
    even permutation as a product of an even number of transpositions
    and pair them. Equal transpositions cancel; two sharing one symbol
    give a three-cycle; a disjoint pair satisfies
    #{(ab)(cd)=(acb)(acd)}. Thus every such pair is a product of
    three-cycles, proving #{[A_d,A_d]=A_d}.}
  }
}

\p{In dimension three the corner character takes values in #{C_3}, the
familiar twist sum. In dimension four the local group has grown to the
nonabelian #{A_4}, yet the character still sees a #{C_3} quotient: all
local changes in #{V} disappear from it. From dimension five onward this
particular corner character sees nothing at all. Other piece types,
permutation signs, and the subgroup generated by legal moves may still
supply constraints. The calculation establishes exactly the loss of this
abelian detector; it does not classify higher-dimensional Rubik groups.}

\p{Transfer connects local residual motion with a global algebraic quantity.
The dimension calculation then exposes a limit of that quantity. This is
one reason to begin with abstract puzzles: it reveals which familiar cube
laws express a general construction and which depend on the local symmetry
groups of a particular dimension.}
